Quoth steve@cheseborough.com (Chezz) on this day of 24 Sep 2003
16:28:09 -0700:

>     In an effort to improve my play, I have been reading some posts
>on this list. And one long one, by a player who seems way beyond my
>level, puzzles me. He advises avoiding combat, and letting the pet
>take care of most of the monsters. Apparently he enjoys great success
>through this style.

You should know that that style is very controversial. The basic idea
is to get down to the Minetown altar at as low an experience level as
possible, in order to be able to buy protection at bargain-basement
prices. One side effect of doing this is that the monsters you meet
are generated partially on the basis of your character's experience
level, and therefore you'll meet easier monsters than you would if you
were at a higher experience level.

As a moderately experienced player (four ascensions) who has tried
this approach, I find that the risks outweigh the possible benefits.
Even if you can buy a bunch of protection early on, you're still left
with few hitpoints several levels deep into the dungeon. Lose the pet
you're depending on so heavily, and you're probably toast.

>     What puzzles me is that even playing basically the opposite of
>that approach -- that is, clobbering almost everything in sight -- I
>often get to the Quest level with my character at too low an
>experience level to qualify for the Quest. 

Most people get to the Quest level before they're ready to qualify for
it. That's no big deal. I think most players simply keep working their
way down the levels toward the Castle, and come back up when they're
ready to do the Quest. In some cases (e.g., the Monk quest), it's
advised to clear out the Castle and become ascension-ready before
doing the Quest.

>Or just barely qualifying
>for it, which I have found is not as good as being a level or two
>higher than that, if you want to survive the Quest.

Survival of the Quest is less dependant upon experience level than it
is upon collecting the necessary resistances, getting a sufficiently
low AC, and raising HP (which can be done in ways other than by
levelling up). My guess is, you find the Quest easier to do when
getting to level 15 or 16 because, in the process of doing that,
you're also gaining some of the items listed above.

>     So, if you play the combat-avoidance style, how do move up the
>levels? 

Generally, players avoiding combat in order to play the "protection
racket" (described above) begin engaging in combat after they've
bought protection and thus begin levelling up normally. Those actually
attempting pacifist conduct have to find alternate means of levelling
up, such as potions of gain level, certain combinations of major/minor
consultations with the Oracle, and wraith corpses.
